  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1051 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nLow clouds are moving in from the north overnight tonight with\ngusty winds out of the north. Looking at the overall pattern, a\ndeep upper-level trough is bringing a surge of northerly winds\ninto the Northern Plains tonight. These winds are amplified by a\nstrengthening pressure gradient as a broad ridge over the\nIntermountain West pushes into the Plains. Winds will be \nstrongest toward daybreak tomorrow morning with northerly winds \ngusting 30 to 40 mph, potentially higher in northeast Nebraska. \nWith windy conditions and temperatures in the upper 30s to low \n40s, we'll see wind chill fall to near or even below freezing \nacross much of our area Friday morning. As the trough shifts off\nto the east and high pressure builds in, winds will relax going\ninto the afternoon with highs warming into the low-to-mid 50s.\n\nThis ridge slides east across the central CONUS bringing a\ntransition back to southwesterly flow late Saturday. Mostly\nclear skies will allow temperatures to fall into the upper 30s \nto low 40s by Sunday morning. As southwesterly flow continues to\nadvect in warmer air, we'll see temperatures climb into the \nmid-to-upper 60s Sunday afternoon with this warming trend \ncontinuing into next week. \n\nWhile the weather remains quiet, the upper-level pattern\ncontinues to be fairly unstable with a series of shortwaves\nmoving through amplifying warm-air advection across our area\nTuesday and Wednesday. Temperatures potentially reach near 80 \nby Wednesday afternoon. We're watching another deep trough \nforecast to slide down the West Coast and deepen through the \nweek next week. Weak shortwaves ahead of the main trough \nWednesday and Thursday could bring a low chance of showers or \nstorms. Our next good chance for more widespread showers and \nstorms looks to be later next week with the arrival of the main \ntrough.",
